  "content": {
    "title": "NC State basketball bounced from March Madness. Here are 4 reasons why",
    "text": "March 18, 2026, 1:08 a.m. ETNC State basketball’s March Madness stay proved shorter than hoped, the Wolfpack losing a hard-fought 68-66 matchup with Texas in the First Four play-in game of the 2026 NCAA men’s tournament.Tramon Mark’s mid-range shot with a second left earned the No. 11 Longhorns a first-round date with West Region 6-seed BYU on Thursday, March 19, in Portland, Oregon.Back in the tournament for the first time since 2024, the Wolfpack were seeking their second Final Four in three seasons. Here are four keys which played a significant role in the Wolfpack’s early ouster.Hitting the 'Mark'Playing in his 169th career game, Mark put his veteran mettle on display down the stretch.Texas’ senior guard finished with a game-high 17 points, though his last four were crucial in the Longhorns remaining alive.With NC State on the comeback trail, Mark hit a 17-foot jumper to give his team a 66-62 lead with 37 seconds left. His final shot will be the one most remembered, however, his mid-range jumper breaking a 66-all tie.Too little, too lateComing into the NCAA tournament, NC State averaged 10.5 3-pointers per game, its 242 shots from deep range this season setting a school record.Such wasn’t evident much of the night against Texas, the Wolfpack struggling from beyond the arc until the game’s latter stages. NC State finished 6-of-16 from 3-point range against Texas, three of those finding the mark in the game’s final 90 seconds.For its troubles from the field, NC State (23-of-59; 39%) actually finished with a better shot percentage than Texas (21-of-57; 37%)..Living at the charity stripeDespite going through its share of droughts from the field, Texas relied heavily on its ability to get to the free throw line throughout Tuesday’s game.The length of Matas Vokietaitis proved problematic for NC State much of the night, doing his part to foul out Wolfpack big men Musa Sagnia and Ven-Allen Lubin. More importantly, his influence stacked NC State’s foul count, Texas shooting 21-of-29 from the free throw line.Meanwhile, NC State hit 14-of-18 free throws in the game.Inability to gather reboundsCorralling rebounds has been an issue for NC State, the Wolfpack ranking 14th in the ACC during the 2025-26 campaign. Such came back to hurt them against Texas, whose size and aggressiveness on the boards further magnified NC State’s glaring weakness. The Longhorns finished plus-12 in the rebound department, with 6-foot-3 guard Chendall Weaver leading them with 10 rebounds — including four on the offensive end of the floor — as part of a double-double.",
